    "lines": [
      {
        "scene": "hook",
        "text": "This Paris Metro train runs on rubber tyres.",
        "start": 0.3,
        "seconds": 3.44,
        "window": 4.03,
        "fill": 0.854
      },
      {
        "scene": "ask",
        "text": "Paris first tried it in 1951. So what keeps it on the track?",
        "start": 4.33,
        "seconds": 4.64,
        "window": 5.1,
        "fill": 0.91
      },
      {
        "scene": "tyres",
        "text": "Under each car, fat tyres roll on two flat running tracks. They carry the weight and drive the train.",
        "start": 9.43,
        "seconds": 6.93,
        "window": 7.37,
        "fill": 0.94
      },
      {
        "scene": "guide",
        "text": "But a tyre can't steer itself. So small sideways wheels press on a guide bar at each side.",
        "start": 16.8,
        "seconds": 6.27,
        "window": 6.63,
        "fill": 0.946
      },
      {
        "scene": "grip",
        "text": "Why the trouble? Rubber grips better than steel, so the train speeds up and brakes harder, and runs quieter.",
        "start": 23.43,
        "seconds": 6.57,
        "window": 7.17,
        "fill": 0.916
      },
      {
        "scene": "steel",
        "text": "And right beside every tyre, on the same axle, sits a steel train wheel, just above an ordinary rail.",
        "start": 30.6,
        "seconds": 7.04,
        "window": 7.53,
        "fill": 0.935
      },
      {
        "scene": "payoff",
        "text": "If a tyre goes flat, that wheel drops onto the rail and the train rolls on. A steel train, hiding inside a rubber one.",
        "start": 38.13,
        "seconds": 8.65,
        "window": 8.97,
        "fill": 0.964
      },
      {
        "scene": "loop",
        "text": "Montreal built its whole metro this way in 1966, and it has carried over 10B trips. Back in Paris, the tyres keep rolling.",
        "start": 47.1,
        "seconds": 10.46,
        "window": 10.83,
        "fill": 0.966
      }
    ]
  },
